$DIR/test.p:3:21: invalid argument: length and capacity swapped
    This error occurs when make is called with three arguments, and its length
    argument is larger than its capacity argument.
    (see "Making channels and maps" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.7)
    (run "pluto explain E0091" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/test.p:3:9: make of a slice is not permitted in Plutonium (a slice is a view and cannot own its array; use vec.Make)
    Plutonium has no make for slices. In Go, make([]T, n) allocates an array
    that the garbage collector frees when no slice refers to it. A Plutonium
    slice is a view of an array that something else owns, so a new array needs
    an owner to free it.
    vec.Make[T](n) from pluto/vec returns a vec.Vec[T] of n zero elements, which
    owns its array and frees it in Free; vec.WithCapacity[T](c) is make([]T, 0,
    c). v.Slice() lends the elements as a slice, and v[i] and len(v) work on the
    Vec itself. For a size known when the program is compiled, an array needs no
    allocation at all.
    Plutonium has no garbage collector and never allocates memory unless the
    program asks for it in the code, so that every cost is visible and the same
    code can run inside a kernel.
    Make a vec.Vec, which owns the array:
        import "pluto/vec"

        v := vec.Make[int](3)
        defer v.Free()
        v.Reserve(2 - 3)
    (see "Making channels and maps" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.7)
    (run "pluto explain E2014" for a longer explanation)
